-- title:
--   Additivity of extreme dominant indices under multiplication
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative domain that is a discrete valuation ring and is complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, let $\varpi \in W$ be irreducible, and let $e \ge 1$ and $q \ge 1$ be natural numbers. Work in the crossing model $R =$ `UVCrossingModel W (ϖ ^ e)`, the quotient of $W[[X_0,X_1]]$ (two-variable multivariate power series) by the ideal generated by $X_0X_1 - \varpi^{e}$, with `mk` the quotient map. Let $x \neq 0$ and $y \neq 0$ be elements of $R$, and let $ab$, $ab'$, $ab''$ be pairs of one-variable power series over $W$ whose second components have vanishing constant coefficient and which represent $x$, $y$ and $xy$ respectively, in the sense that `mk (ϖ ^ e)` applied to $\mathrm{inU}(\cdot_1) + \mathrm{inV}(\cdot_2)$ — the series $\sum_i a_i X_0^i + \sum_{j\ge 1} b_j X_1^{j}$ — equals $x$, $y$, $xy$. Fix $p \in \mathbb{N}$ and measure orders by $w \mapsto q \cdot \mathrm{addVal}_W(w)$ in $\mathbb{N}_\infty$. For such data, `dominantIndices` at parameters $E = q e$ and $t = p$ is the set of $n \in \mathbb{Z}$ whose term order $q\,\mathrm{addVal}_W(\mathrm{nfCoeff}\,ab\,n) + \mathrm{annulusWeight}\,(qe)\,p\,(\mathrm{nfExponent}\,n)$ attains `repGaussOrder`, the infimum over all multi-degrees $d$ of $q\,\mathrm{addVal}_W$ of the $d$-th coefficient plus $\mathrm{annulusWeight}\,(qe)\,p\,d$. The conclusion is the conjunction of two statements: if $p + 1 \le q e$, then the infimum of the dominant index set of $ab''$ equals the sum of those of $ab$ and $ab'$; and if $1 \le p \le q e$, then the supremum of the dominant index set of $ab''$ equals the sum of those of $ab$ and $ab'$.
--
--   This is the multiplicativity of initial forms on each circle $v(U) = p/q$ of the annulus $0 \le v(U) \le e$, expressed through the extreme (smallest and largest) dominant indices, which record the one-sided slopes of the Gauss polygon; consequently the difference $\sup - \inf$ is additive as well. It is used in the analysis of characteristic polynomials and leading residues over the crossing model, and in the computation of the circle index drop as a sum of rank times length contributions.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_UVCrossingModel_sInf_dominantIndices_mul_and_sSup_dominantIndices_mul.lean

